# ApexYard

> Use AI coding agents with a controlled engineering process. ApexYard provides automatic code review, release checks, and one view of all managed projects. It is open source and uses plain Markdown and shell.

## What ApexYard is

ApexYard adds the reviews, guardrails, and process behind work driven by your AI coding harness. Claude Code is the canonical hook runtime; adapters connect Codex, OpenCode, Pi, and Cursor. Find common mistakes before release. Review records show what the reviewer checked. 66 skills grouped by what you are trying to do, plus a persistent decision-log across the whole portfolio.

## What ApexYard does

- **Automatic code review.** A reviewer checks each change before it can ship. The review covers the changed code and its system impact.
- **Launch-readiness checks.** Find out what is missing across security, performance, accessibility, monitoring, docs before customers tell you
- **One inbox across all your products.** Every PR, issue, comment, blocker in one screen, no tab-juggling
- **Evidence-grounded quality gates.** Review records cite the source they checked, and quality regression can run across Claude, Cursor, Codex, pi, and opencode.
- **Flexible tracker setup.** Keep issue tracking and code review on different hosts when your workflow needs it.
- **Clear technical writing.** New artifacts use short, consistent language while keeping the decision context.
- **Specialised review roles.** A security-sensitive change gets a security review. A data change gets a review of migration and rollback risk. Each role has a defined scope and checklist.
- **Guardrails that fire themselves.** 60 small scripts catch ticket-first edits, missing rollback plans, secrets in code, red CI before merge — so you do not have to remember
- **A decision-log that follows you.** Every meaningful technical decision is recorded with reasoning — search it next year, the answer's there

## Who can use ApexYard

- **Solo founders shipping with AI** — building alone, need the guardrails a team would give you
- **Non-technical founders managing contractors** — need to know the work is solid without reading every commit
- **Small teams with no engineering process yet** — first version shipped, now you need real review + testing + release discipline

## What you need to start

1. Fork `github.com/me2resh/apexyard` on GitHub
2. Clone the fork locally — it IS your ops repo, no nested installs, no symlinks
3. Run `/setup` in Claude Code — three exchanges to configure company, team, and tech stack
4. Register projects via `/handover <repo>` for adoption, or `/idea` to capture new concepts

## Proof — from our own GitHub history

ApexYard is built using ApexYard. Verifiable from `github.com/me2resh/apexyard`:

- **304 PRs reviewed and merged in the last 90 days**, under the same guardrails the tool installs in your fork
- **36 releases shipped** (v0.1.0 through v5.6.3)
- **144 technical decisions recorded** (released to main) — every meaningful call in an audit trail
- The v5.6 release line adds framework-level adapter defaults; v5.6.3 adds centralized hook dispatch, stricter Rex review validation, and migration coverage.

## See a day with it

Read [how it works](https://apexyard.ai/how-it-works) for the short explanation. Use the site examples to inspect the commands, checks, and evidence behind one governed change.

## Get started in three steps

### 1. Star, fork, clone

```bash
gh repo fork me2resh/apexyard --clone
cd apexyard
git remote add upstream https://github.com/me2resh/apexyard.git
```

### 2. Claude, /setup

```bash
cd apexyard
claude
/setup
# three questions → onboarding.yaml is configured
```

### 3. Adopt, or originate

- `/handover <repo-url>` — existing project → assessment + registry + architecture stub
- `/idea` — new concept → ideas-backlog.md, optional GitHub issue

## What you actually get when you fork it

### Reviews from specialised engineering roles

The review role follows the change. A security-sensitive change gets a security review. A data change gets a review of migration and rollback risk. Each role has a defined scope and checklist.

### Tools for every step of shipping software · 66 slash commands

Want to adopt a project you have inherited? `/handover`. Want to capture an idea before it evaporates? `/idea`. Want to know what is waiting on you across every product? `/inbox`. Want a launch-readiness check before customers see it? `/launch-check`. 66 of these in total — each one is a focused workflow with safety rails.

### Guardrails that stop bad code from shipping · 60 hooks

Every edit must have a ticket. Every database change must have a rollback plan. Every merge must have a review. Secrets in code get caught before they are committed. Mechanically enforced — 60 small scripts that fire at the right moments.

### Reviewers that do not rubber-stamp · 23 agents

- **Rex** (code review) reads what changed and how the rest of the system reacts to it
- **Hakim** (security) flags auth / crypto / secrets
- **Munir** (deps) scans vulnerabilities + licences
- **Tariq** (PR manager) and **Idris** (ticket manager) handle the orchestration

### One inbox across everything

`apexyard.projects.yaml` at your fork root lists every repo you manage. Skills like `/inbox`, `/status`, `/tasks`, `/stakeholder-update` aggregate across it. One prompt, every project. Add a project with `/handover`; sync with upstream via `/update`.

### Drop-in GitHub Actions · 7 ready-made pipelines

At `golden-paths/pipelines/`: `ci.yml`, `code-quality.yml`, `security.yml`, `dependency-audit.yml`, `pr-title-check.yml`, `review-check.yml`, `seo-check.yml`. Copy what you need into each project's `.github/workflows/`.

## Manifesto

Every pull request ties to a ticket. Every ticket has acceptance criteria. Code review validates against those criteria, and QA verifies the result in a separate pass before the ticket closes.

